Link to game: [https://jazzswush.itch.io/project-2-simple-platformer](https://jazzswush.itch.io/project-2-simple-platformer)

For this project I created a simple platformer game with a chance for the player to die or win. I made it simple so I could learn how to code in C# and I was able to implement this on how the player moves and interact with certain things. Player movement like running and jumping have both separate C# code and the player collision with obstacles and the end state has it's own C# coding as well. I used the player movement on the quick tutorial, but I wanted to add more to it. So, I watched a tutorial playlist to follow along to create what I have currently. I did use generative AI to help understand the code and help with console log errors that i did not understand. The tutorial was a bit out of date to the current Unity, so I had to get some help to change some code to fit the current update mechanics. I chose this creative direction because I wanted something simple and free and I was able to find these assets on the store. It was a good starting point to learn and  build this game. I did encounter a lot of challenges, especially understadning the code while going through tutorials. I implemented a wall jumping mechanic, so when players see a wall they can wall jaump and also has wall slide too. But as I was troubleshooting it, the wall jump was working but if a player were to continuously run at the wall, they would be able to keep jumping while hugging the wall. I could not figure out how to fix this, so I just reverted it back to just jumping and having wall slides. If i were to add something, I would have added collectables, enemies, and health bars to the game. I just did not have enough time, but i am proud still at what i was able to make and accomplish. I have still much to learn and understand C#, but i am slowly getting the hang of it. Overall, I learned the basics of C# code and how to implement them into a player for movements. Now, I just need to retain this information and keep working at it. I wish I did a better job at documenting and taking screenshots to show my progress. Especially, when I was building up my code to what it is currently.
